VIOLATION — the judge should return a non-empty violation list. Naming any of the four is a correct
find; all four are semantic defects sitting inside structurally complete furniture.

  1. Prohibitions, not encouragements. The clause heading reads "Should Generally Be Kept Aligned"
     and the body is built from permissive modals: "should generally", "are encouraged to", "where
     practical", "good practice to consider", "use judgment about how much verification is
     proportionate". No MUST or BLOCKED sits in a load-bearing position, so nothing is testable and
     no reviewer could say whether a given diff complies.

  2. BLOCKED corpus is abstract, not verbatim. The heading is present, but under it sits a
     description of a CATEGORY of excuse — "rationalize ... in various ways", "reasoning of this
     general kind" — with no quotable string. A linguistic tripwire needs a string for an agent's
     own prose to match; there is none. The heading's presence is the shape cue, the emptiness
     beneath it is the defect.

  3. Why lines exceed the two-sentence bound and restate the clause. The clause's `**Why:**` runs to
     a single heavily-subordinated sentence spanning five clauses, and the MUST NOT's Why defers to
     "the reasons discussed at greater length in the preceding section" rather than naming a failure
     mode.

  4. The DO / DO-NOT block is inert. Both arms are comments restating the prose ("Review the scope
     list and make sure it is correct" versus "Do not allow the scope list to drift"). Neither is a
     command, neither could be run, and the two do not differ in what they would do.

What this pole gets RIGHT, and the judge must not read as compliance: the frontmatter is well-formed,
the Wiring block carries its fields, Origin is present and cites an incident, and the section
skeleton matches its paired candidate exactly. That is the point of the pair — every structural
element the meta-rules require is PRESENT, so a judge checking for presence finds a complete artifact.
